`aplan export-agents-md` writes the half of an agent contract that the plan and
its policy already decide (`A1-038`). The first pilot enforced its plan in CI
while nothing told an agent the rule existed, and the file it then hand-wrote is
a second, ungated copy of the policy -- which by the time this shipped had drifted:
it told agents the MCP surface was "exactly four tools", and it is eight.

The block is fenced, because an agent file is also where a project says things
`aplan` knows nothing about. `--write` replaces what is between the markers and
leaves the rest alone; with no markers it appends rather than prepends, since a
file that opens with a generated block reads as a generated file.

`--check` is the gate shape `gen-schema --check` and `gen-roadmap --check` have.
Stale content between the markers is what it exists to catch, and it names the
command that repairs it rather than the mistake. (`--write` itself is exercised in
a sandbox rather than here: a writing command run against its own fixture edits
the fixture, and the corpus would then be testing whatever the last run left.)

```
$ aplan export-agents-md --check AGENTS.md
? 1
AGENTS.md disagrees with `aplan export-agents-md`, checked by aplan [..]. Regenerate it with `aplan export-agents-md --write AGENTS.md`.

```

What `--write` would put there is this repository's policy in its own terms,
never the tool's defaults: the plan's location, its own id convention, and the
evidence its `.aplan.yml` actually requires.

```
$ aplan export-agents-md
<!-- aplan:begin -- generated by `aplan export-agents-md`; edits between these markers are lost -->
...
<!-- aplan:end -->

```
